alex.m: Heads up — I'm putting together the JS SDK shim. Need a final call on our fetch strategy across Node (CJS/ESM), Edge, and browsers. Undici vs global fetch? Also typing and packaging concerns. Want minimal footprint for web, deterministic behavior for Node. Thoughts?

sara.eng: Short: prefer global fetch where available. Use optional undici import in Node fallback. Packaging gets tricky w/ undici ESM-only and CJS consumers though.

tim_o: Agree global-first. For Node >=18 it's fine, but older LTSs exist. Suggest runtime-detect + graceful polyfill install. Example:

```js
// runtime-fetch.js
export async function getFetch() {
  if (typeof fetch === 'function') return fetch;
  try {
    const { fetch: undiciFetch } = await import('undici');
    return undiciFetch;
  } catch (e) {
    throw new Error('No fetch available — please install undici or use node >=18');
  }
}
```

sara.eng: Also recommend we export a tiny wrapper that normalizes Response/headers/AbortController differences and exposes typed `FetchLike` so TS users get consistent types.

shreyas: In sample-apps we ship `dist/fetch-shim.js` and a `dist/fetch-shim.d.ts`. Use conditional exports in package.json, e.g.:

```json
{
  "main": "./dist/index.cjs.js",
  "module": "./dist/index.esm.js",
  "types": "./dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "import": "./dist/index.esm.js",
      "require": "./dist/index.cjs.js"
    }
  }
}
```

lina: Edge runtimes (Vercel/Cloudflare/Deno) have global fetch but different streaming semantics and headers. Don't rely on undici-specific APIs there — add a runtime flag so we can route to a `web` variant.

tim_o: Important for auth/retry: our retry middleware needs response clone/body. Streaming responses cannot be retried unless buffered. For retries either only retry idempotent requests or buffer up to N bytes. Also abort/signal handling must be preserved across retries.

```js
// simple retry sketch
async function fetchWithRetry(fetchFn, req, retries=1) {
  for (let i=0;i<=retries;i++){
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const signal = controller.signal;
    try {
      return await fetchFn(req.url, { ...req, signal });
    } catch (e) {
      if (i===retries) throw e;
      // refresh token before retry if 401-timeout style
      await maybeRefreshToken();
    }
  }
}
```

sara.eng: For onboarding: on Node ESM users (node <18) instruct `npm install undici`. For CJS show the require hack: `const { fetch } = require('undici');` — include that in quickstart templates.

devex-bot: FYI internal doc: https://intra.redwood/devex/http-runtime-guidance — queued for updates with snippets.

alex.m: Packaging question: should we bundle a ~20KB `universalFetch` wrapper so customers get zero-config, or leave zero footprint and require installing undici for Node <18? Token refresh + retry concerns push me to a small default wrapper.

shreyas: We decided to make it opt-in in sample apps: SDK exposes `init({ fetch?: typeof fetch })` so apps can inject runtime fetch. Default tries runtime-detect + auto-import undici. Keeps browser bundles tiny.

lina: On types: publish explicit type helper `types/fetch.d.ts` with `export type FetchLike = typeof fetch;` and re-export it from root. Helps consumers with isolatedModules and TS project references.

tim_o: Note bundlers: dynamic import of undici can be tree-shaken weirdly. The `try { await import('undici') } catch {}` pattern works but add tests for rollup/webpack/esbuild. Test matrix: node14/16/18, deno, vercel-edge, cf-workers.

alex.m: OK — plan for PR: (1) default init with runtime-detect + dynamic undici import, (2) opt-in `init({fetch})` injection, (3) small `fetch-shim` that normalizes headers/Response/AbortController, (4) docs + sample-app snippets for CJS/ESM. Will add retry streaming note. Who wants to co-author?

sara.eng: I'll take the types + package.json exports updates.

shreyas: I'll add the CJS/ESM sample-app updates and CI matrix.

devex-bot: :white_check_mark: Created task DEVEX-482: "SDK universal fetch strategy + docs"

alex.m: Thanks — I'll open the PR and tag you both. Follow-up in this thread for code review.
